comments.message:
Bit of a nit, but can you please collapse these into one condition, and test them in the same order as the formal parameters?


```suggestion
	if (!pdr_buf || !iter) {
		return -EINVAL;
	}
```

However, also take into account the comments from the header.

So going back to some of the discussion on the header, this would not be necessary if we take `struct pldm_state_sensor_pdr *pdr` as an argument to the function. You can instead initialise the msgbuf with `possible_states` as the buffer argument, and use `pdr->composite_sensor_count` as the size argument.

Actually, looking further down, none of that would be necessary. We'd initialise the iterator object directly from the information we already have available:

```
...

if (pdr_size < sizeof(struct pldm_state_sensor_pdr)) {
    return -EOVERFLOW;
}

...

iter->cursor = pdr->possible_states;
iter->remaining = pdr_size - sizeof(struct pldm_state_sensor_pdr);
iter->total_count = pdr->composite_effecter_count;
iter->current_index = 0;

return 0;
```

```suggestion
	struct pldm_pdr_state_effecter_iter iter = {0};
```
